      <title>Self-fixing AI agents: already here?</title>
      <link>https://neuraltrust.ai/blog/self-fixing-ai-agents</link>
      <description>&lt;div style="border: 1px solid #ddd; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px; margin: 8px 0; background-color: #f9f9f9;"&gt;
&lt;div style="font-weight: bold; color: #2c3e50; margin-bottom: 8px;"&gt;
📄 Source: NeuralTrust Blog
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 8px;"&gt;
&lt;span style="color: #7f8c8d;"&gt;👤 Author: Rodrigo Fernández&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span style="color: #7f8c8d;"&gt;📅 Date: October 16, 2025&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin-bottom: 8px;"&gt;
&lt;span style="background-color: #3498db; color: white; padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 3px; font-size: 0.8em;"&gt;Artificial Intelligence&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="background-color: #3498db; color: white; padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 3px; font-size: 0.8em;"&gt;Machine Learning&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="background-color: #3498db; color: white; padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 3px; font-size: 0.8em;"&gt;AI Adaptability&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="line-height: 1.6;"&gt;
The article explores a recent observation where an OpenAI model, o3, exhibited behavior akin to self-debugging by iteratively diagnosing and adjusting its inputs to fix errors without explicit programming to do so. This adaptive behavior, seen after the release of GPT-5, suggests a future where AI systems may autonomously manage and correct their own operational issues, marking a shift from deterministic responses to more flexible problem-solving approaches.
&lt;/div&gt;</description>
